{
  "requirement": "Every {term}`requirements.sdk.graph.criteria.criterion` type implements\n`trust_but_verify::graph::Criterion<D>` for its\n{term}`requirements.sdk.application_domain`.\n\n```rust,ignore\npub trait Criterion<D: Domain>: Send + Sized {\n    type Ok: Serialize + DeserializeOwned + Send;\n\n    fn source_node_id(&self) -> D::NodeId;\n    fn criterion_path(&self) -> CriterionPath;\n    fn version(&self) -> CriterionVersion;\n    fn execute(\n        self,\n        ctx: &mut ValidationContext<'_, D>,\n    ) -> impl Future<Output = Result<Self::Ok, ValidationError<D::Error>>> + Send;\n}\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligation.** The requirement says every criterion type implements `Criterion<D>` for its application domain. The trait is `Send + Sized`. `Ok` must be `Serialize + DeserializeOwned + Send`. The trait has four members: `source_node_id`, `criterion_path`, `version` and an async `execute` that returns `Result<Self::Ok, ValidationError<D::Error>>`. Most of this is a compile-time contract. The behaviour of each member is covered by refining requirements (`criterion_trait.source_node_id`, `.version`, `.answer`, `execute`, `criterion_path`). Those are not reassessed here.\n\n**Production implementor.** The only non-test criterion type is `Review`, in `src/authoring/sdk.rs#impl[Review/Criterion<ReviewGraph<'_>>]`. Its `criterion_path` and `version` come from `crate::sdk::review_criterion` and `crate::sdk::review_version`.\n\n**Tests mapped to the obligation:**\n- `src/authoring/sdk.rs#mod[tests]/fn[review_graph_persists_and_reuses_a_negative_validation_through_the_sdk]` drives the real `Review` through `Validator`.\n  - It asserts `source_node_id()` equals the node, `criterion_path().segments()` is `[\"review\"]`, and `version()` is `REVIEW_VERSION`.\n  - It runs `verify`, which calls `execute`, then stores and reloads the evidence.\n  - This shows the real criterion type fulfils the whole trait contract and works end to end with the domain.\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/contracts.rs#fn[criteria_keep_typed_answers_and_persist_only_when_requested]` uses the generic `Review<O>` implementor.\n  - It checks that execution returns the typed `Ok` value, that the source node and path are carried through, and that the answer round-trips through serialization (the `Serialize + DeserializeOwned` bounds).\n  - It uses `Ok` types `Assessment`, `Vec<String>` and `()`.\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/contracts.rs#fn[validation_and_answer_need_send_without_sync]` uses `Cell<u32>` as `Ok`. That type is `Send` but not `Sync`, so it checks the `Send`-only bound on `Ok` and the `Send` future from `execute`.\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/tests.rs#fn[deterministic_validation_uses_required_storage_without_an_agent]` and `crates/trust-but-verify/src/graph/validation_tests.rs#fn[caller_reuses_negative_answers_and_compares_revisions_explicitly]` exercise additional implementors (`Review` for `Memory`).\n  - They assert execution output, the stored record and version compatibility.\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]` is marked with the SDK public-API requirements, not this one.\n\n**Other implementors.** The extra test-only implementors (`ParseAnswer`, `StoredReview`, `ReadSource`, `Question` and others) also compile against the trait.\n\n**Gaps.** Compilation of these implementors with the stated bounds, plus runtime assertions on each trait member's use, is as much verification as a trait-shape requirement allows. I found no plausible violating behaviour that these tests would accept. There are no `{fixme}` acknowledgments and none are needed.",
  "references": "56f4b9fe7da0aa15014b3838508c84537c402f91531e0b341d3e3ebb1b623825",
